Subject: [PATCH 05814/11362] [outer-loop] run the history-conditioned TRM
 pilot: result contradicts, not confirms

Ran the recommended next step from the frame-only pilot: same held-out
game (ft09), same two architectures, added an 8-action history window
(cheap change, per the honest recommendation from the first run). Also
added a repeat-last-action heuristic baseline for context.

Result, reported without spin:

- Non-recursive baseline jumped from 0.4626 (frame-only) to 0.7757 with
  history -- confirms the v1 hypothesis that recent action history was
  the missing signal.
- BUT the recursive refiner now scores 0.6107 -- 16.5pp WORSE than the
  non-recursive baseline, despite near-identical training accuracy
  (0.6998 vs 0.7026). This is the OPPOSITE ranking from the frame-only
  run, where recursion won by 15.3pp under nearly the same
  training-accuracy parity.

This reversal, not either single run, is the important finding. Both
pilots used one fixed seed, three epochs, and a standalone
reimplementation far simpler than nano-trm's own validated training
regime (no ACT halting, no tuned per-architecture hyperparameters) -- a
plausible, honest explanation for a 30-point swing in the
recursive-vs-non-recursive ranking between two closely related
conditions, rather than evidence that recursion genuinely helps or
hurts depending on task framing.

Revised state, documented in the "v2 update" section of
docs/research-notes/trm-leave-one-game-out-pilot-results-2026-07-05.md:
inconclusive, with contradictory single-run signals in both directions.
Before any further conclusion, this needs multiple seeds per arm and/or
a properly-tuned per-architecture training regime -- not another
single-seed run under a third task framing.

